Yes, absolutely. Here's exactly how:

---

## The Approach — NetInfo + Channel Status

Two signals combined tell you whether Realtime is healthy:

1. **`@react-native-community/netinfo`** — already in your dependencies — tells you if the device has network connectivity at all
2. **Supabase channel subscription status** — the `.subscribe((status) => ...)` callback on `dispatch:lagos` already fires with `'SUBSCRIBED'`, `'CLOSED'`, `'CHANNEL_ERROR'`, `'TIMED_OUT'`

You combine them into a single ref: `isRealtimeHealthy`. The 15s poll only runs when that ref is `false`.

---

## The Logic

```
device offline          → isRealtimeHealthy = false → poll runs
device online but...
  channel = SUBSCRIBED  → isRealtimeHealthy = true  → poll paused
  channel = CLOSED      → isRealtimeHealthy = false → poll runs
  channel = TIMED_OUT   → isRealtimeHealthy = false → poll runs
  channel = CHANNEL_ERROR → isRealtimeHealthy = false → poll runs
```

---

## Concretely in the code

**Step 1** — Track channel health in a ref alongside the existing `isOnline` state:

```ts
const isRealtimeHealthyRef = useRef(false);
```

**Step 2** — In the existing Realtime subscription effect (line 196), update the ref inside the `.subscribe()` callback:

```ts
.subscribe((status) => {
  isRealtimeHealthyRef.current = status === 'SUBSCRIBED';
  console.log('[DoctorLayout] dispatch:lagos status:', status);
});
```

**Step 3** — Change the 15s poll effect to check the ref before syncing:

```ts
const id = setInterval(() => {
  if (!isRealtimeHealthyRef.current) {
    console.log('[DoctorLayout] Poll tick — Realtime unhealthy, force-syncing');
    forceSyncRef.current();
  } else {
    console.log('[DoctorLayout] Poll tick — Realtime healthy, skipping');
  }
}, 15000);
```

---

## Why a ref and not state?

If you used `useState` for `isRealtimeHealthy`, changing it would re-run the poll effect (because it'd be in the dependency array), tearing down and recreating the interval every time the channel status changes. A ref holds the value without triggering re-renders or effect re-runs — the interval stays stable and just reads the current value on each tick.

---

## One extra improvement worth adding at the same time

Supabase's JS client has a built-in `onOpen`/`onClose` on the socket level via `supabase.realtime.onOpen` and `supabase.realtime.onClose`. You could also trigger an **immediate** `forceSync` the moment the socket reconnects (not waiting up to 15s), so the doctor's queue is fresh the instant connectivity is restored:

```ts
supabase.realtime.onOpen(() => {
  isRealtimeHealthyRef.current = true;
  forceSyncRef.current(); // immediate resync on reconnect
});
supabase.realtime.onClose(() => {
  isRealtimeHealthyRef.current = false;
});
```

This gives you the best of both worlds: **zero unnecessary polls when healthy, immediate resync when recovering**.

