## Audit findings relevant to the 422 error

**The `accountReference` formula (identical in both functions):**
```typescript
const accountReference = `FL-${requesterId.replace(/-/g, '').slice(0, 20)}`;
```
A UUID like `24cdb480-e9a5-49e7-82fd-9fc37b47eff3` → stripped of hyphens → `24cdb480e9a549e782fd` (20 chars) → final ref: `FL-24cdb480e9a549e782fd` (22 chars total).

**The GET-before-POST logic:**
The GET is called at `GET /api/v2/bank-transfer/reserved-accounts/${accountReference}`. It only falls through to POST if `!getRes.ok || !getData.requestSuccessful`. 

**Root cause of the 422:** The GET is failing (returning non-OK or `requestSuccessful: false`) even though the account already exists at Monnify, so the code falls through to POST and Monnify rejects it with "You cannot reserve more than 1 account(s) for a customer." This means the GET is not successfully retrieving the existing account — likely because the Monnify token obtained just before is scoped to a different contract/environment, or the GET endpoint returns a non-2xx for an existing account under certain conditions (e.g. a 404 with `requestSuccessful: false` in the body when the account exists but under a different contract code). The fix would be to treat a Monnify 422 on POST as a signal to retry the GET, or to check the GET response body more carefully (some Monnify environments return 200 with `requestSuccessful: false` for found accounts).

