## Audit Summary

### (a) Event names
| Function | Event name |
|---|---|
| `start-shift` | `SHIFT_STARTED` |
| `pause-shift` | `SHIFT_PAUSED` |
| `resume-shift` | `SHIFT_RESUMED` |

### (b) Broadcast channels
| Function | Channels |
|---|---|
| `start-shift` | `session:{session_id}` **and** `doctor:{doctor_id}` |
| `pause-shift` | `session:{session_id}` **and** `doctor:{doctor_id}` |
| `resume-shift` | `session:{session_id}` **only** — ⚠️ does NOT broadcast to `doctor:{doctor_id}` |

### (c) Payload shape — does it include the full session with `doctor_phone` / `requester_phone`?

| Function | Payload |
|---|---|
| `start-shift` | `{ session: updated }` — the full `updated` row from `coverage_sessions` via `.select('*')` |
| `pause-shift` | ⚠️ **Partial** — `{ session_id, status, current_day, day_logs, day_cost_naira }` — **no `session` object at all** |
| `resume-shift` | `{ session: updated }` — the full `updated` row from `coverage_sessions` via `.select('*')` |

**Critical finding:** `pause-shift` is the outlier. It broadcasts a hand-crafted partial payload with no `session` object, so `doctor_phone` and `requester_phone` are **absent** from its broadcast. `start-shift` and `resume-shift` both broadcast `{ session: updated }` which will include those fields if they exist as columns on `coverage_sessions` (since both use `.select('*')`). Whether those columns actually exist on the table is a separate question — but structurally, `start-shift` and `resume-shift` would carry them while `pause-shift` would not.

