## SQL Query Results

### `requester_profiles` — columns in (`organisation_name`, `first_name`, `last_name`, `full_name`)

| column_name | data_type |
|---|---|
| organisation_name | text |

**Only `organisation_name` exists.** There is no `first_name`, `last_name`, or `full_name` column on `requester_profiles`.

---

### `profiles` — columns in (`organisation_name`, `first_name`, `last_name`, `full_name`)

| column_name | data_type |
|---|---|
| first_name | text |
| last_name | text |

**`first_name` and `last_name` exist. `organisation_name` and `full_name` do not exist on `profiles`.**

---

## Summary of the Critical Discrepancy

`accept-request` builds `requester_name` by reading `first_name` and `last_name` from the **`profiles`** table for the requester. However, `requester_profiles` has **no** `first_name` or `last_name` — it only has `organisation_name`. The `profiles` table does have `first_name`/`last_name`, so the query works — but if a requester is an organisation, their name in `profiles` may be blank while their actual name lives in `requester_profiles.organisation_name`, which is never consulted for the `requester_name` field.

